00:07:38.134 --> 00:07:39.300
So what is Transfer Service?

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:41.820
Transfer Service is not a
tool that you can install,

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:43.380
it is a Cloud-hosted tool.

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:45.860
So you go to our Cloud Console.

00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:48.620
You can find it under
the Storage section,

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:51.810
under called
Transfer, obviously.

00:07:51.810 --> 00:07:53.520
When you click on
Transfer, you will

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:55.020
get a list of transfer jobs.

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:58.530
And then if you click on
the Create Transfer Job,

00:07:58.530 --> 00:08:03.380
you can then create
a transfer job.

00:08:03.380 --> 00:08:06.720
So step one would be
you specify your source.

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:11.360
So it could be a Cloud Google,
GCS bucket, or S3 bucket.

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:15.125
We can actually transfer
from HTTP, HTTPS sources.

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:20.270
And then you can kind
of specify conditions,

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:22.800
so transfer files only
with certain prefixes,

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:25.170
or exclude files with
certain prefixes.

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:27.910
How many of you know
like why prefix is

00:08:27.910 --> 00:08:30.300
so important in object storage?

00:08:33.030 --> 00:08:33.835
Not many.

00:08:33.835 --> 00:08:37.230
So objects storage is kind
of like a flat storage.

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:40.500
So there's no concept of
folder, like physical folders

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:41.620
and directories.

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:43.860
So a lot of customers
they use prefixes

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:47.400
to kind of identifying folders.

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:50.370
So for example, in
your local file system,

00:08:50.370 --> 00:08:55.020
you may have
A/B/C/object or file,

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:59.470
where the slashes kind of denote
you're [INAUDIBLE] limitations.

00:08:59.470 --> 00:09:04.430
So when you import that file
into GCS, it will be named,

00:09:04.430 --> 00:09:08.830
for example, A/B/C/file
name, or object name.

00:09:08.830 --> 00:09:13.020
So the directory prefix
becomes part of the file

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:14.550
name or the object name.

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:17.610
And so by using these
prefix conditions,

00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:22.530
you can say that OK, I'm going
to transfer only certain files

00:09:22.530 --> 00:09:24.770
and certain folders or
directories into GCS.

00:09:28.770 --> 00:09:32.430
So another way of defining
source would be Amazon S3.

00:09:32.430 --> 00:09:37.050
So you define the, enter the
name of the S3 bucket, access

00:09:37.050 --> 00:09:39.090
key ID, and the
secret access key,

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:44.240
and all those things you can
get from the Amazon console.

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:47.510
So step two is where you
identify the destination.

00:09:47.510 --> 00:09:51.890
And you do have transfer
options where you override

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:54.890
the destination if it already
exists, or delete objects

00:09:54.890 --> 00:09:59.240
at the source, or
you delete objects

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:01.990
if the source object
has been deleted.

00:10:01.990 --> 00:10:05.059
So it's kind of like a
synchronization mechanism.

00:10:05.059 --> 00:10:06.600
And then finally,
step three is where

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:08.720
you configured to transfer.

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:12.370
You can run it now or you
can run it at a later time,

00:10:12.370 --> 00:10:13.904
on a daily basis.

00:10:13.904 --> 00:10:15.320
And then when you
click on Create,

00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:19.810
and it creates a transfer job,
it gets queued up for running.

00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:21.790
So that's Transfer
Service for you.

00:10:21.790 --> 00:10:25.090
Obviously tips, I think
there's a recap of earlier.

00:10:25.090 --> 00:10:30.770
You can do all these things
and you can create basically

00:10:30.770 --> 00:10:32.810
24 transfer jobs,
one hour apart,

00:10:32.810 --> 00:10:36.590
so that gets you an
hourly syncing mechanism.

00:10:36.590 --> 00:10:38.910
All right so we'll talk
about Transfer Service.

00:10:38.910 --> 00:10:40.710
So what if your
data is in the colo,

00:10:40.710 --> 00:10:44.230
or in your own data center, but
with good network connectivity.

00:10:44.230 --> 00:10:47.680
So for that, we
have a tool, gsutil.

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:50.070
It's all lower case.

00:10:50.070 --> 00:10:52.830
So gsutil is our primary
command line tool.

00:10:52.830 --> 00:10:56.650
It runs on Windows, Linux, Mac.

00:10:56.650 --> 00:10:58.710
It can run on a GCE instance.

00:10:58.710 --> 00:11:01.370
You could even run it on a
EC2 instance, if you like.

00:11:01.370 --> 00:11:04.290
It's actively maintain,
actively supported,

00:11:04.290 --> 00:11:07.390
Unix friendly syntax,
lots of documentation.

00:11:07.390 --> 00:11:12.630
There's also a Stack
Overflow discussion.

00:11:12.630 --> 00:11:16.270
So performance, gsutil
can run multi-threaded,

00:11:16.270 --> 00:11:19.060
so you can have
multiple processes that

00:11:19.060 --> 00:11:21.430
will upload data in parallel.

00:11:21.430 --> 00:11:28.600
If you have lots of
like large data files,

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:31.120
gsutil can break
up the data files

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:34.840
into chunks to upload
the chunks in parallel.

00:11:34.840 --> 00:11:38.170
And then once uploaded, it will
reconstitute the original file

00:11:38.170 --> 00:11:39.500
and delete the chunks.

00:11:39.500 --> 00:11:41.620
So that's where there's
a caution there.

00:11:41.620 --> 00:11:45.030
If you are uploading like
using this feature to upload

00:11:45.030 --> 00:11:47.470
to Nearline or Coldline,
when you delete the chunks

00:11:47.470 --> 00:11:52.670
you do incur what we call
Early Deletion Penalty.

00:11:52.670 --> 00:11:55.460
So download has
the same benefits.

00:11:55.460 --> 00:11:58.890
So retry, so we do have retry
logic for transient network

00:11:58.890 --> 00:11:59.994
failures.

00:11:59.994 --> 00:12:01.160
There are more details here.

00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:04.457
I'm not going to go into
each and every line.

00:12:04.457 --> 00:12:06.540
And then there's something
called a manifest file.

00:12:06.540 --> 00:12:10.040
So we can generate a manifest
file automatically when we run.

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:11.540
And the manifest
file will indicate,

00:12:11.540 --> 00:12:14.604
you can see the example there,
of which objects or files were

00:12:14.604 --> 00:12:16.770
successfully transferred,
and then failures as well.

00:12:16.770 --> 00:12:20.060
All And the manifest file
itself can be used as an input

00:12:20.060 --> 00:12:25.160
into gsutil, so you can do
an autoretry of failures.

00:12:25.160 --> 00:12:27.150
So a lot of customers
use it that way.

00:12:27.150 --> 00:12:31.020
In a lot of cases, because we
are now running HTTP requests,

00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:34.160
sometimes there are failures.

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:36.990
So for whatever reason,
a file is not uploaded.

00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:39.530
So you can use the
manifest files in input

00:12:39.530 --> 00:12:42.610
to make sure that if you have a
million files, that all of them

00:12:42.610 --> 00:12:45.960
are actually
uploaded effectively.

00:12:45.960 --> 00:12:47.430
There's also resumability.

00:12:47.430 --> 00:12:50.220
So there's automatic
for large objects.

00:12:50.220 --> 00:12:54.000
And then there's the
resume file that we

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:57.430
use to track like
uploads in process.

00:12:57.430 --> 00:13:01.500
So if there's block
for whatever reason,

00:13:01.500 --> 00:13:05.550
we can do a resume
using this data.

00:13:05.550 --> 00:13:09.350
So tips and tricks, I think that
the key one is the first bullet

00:13:09.350 --> 00:13:09.900
point.

00:13:09.900 --> 00:13:13.130
So if you have lots of
data, a huge data set,

00:13:13.130 --> 00:13:15.180
you want the partition
that data set and use

00:13:15.180 --> 00:13:17.200
multiple host for transfers.

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:20.680
Multiple host being like
physical machines or VMs.

00:13:20.680 --> 00:13:24.900
And each one will run its
own instance of gsutil,

00:13:24.900 --> 00:13:28.820
uploading a section
of your data source.

00:13:28.820 --> 00:13:31.220
What this does is that
then you effectively has

00:13:31.220 --> 00:13:34.190
a whole bunch of
gsutil instances

00:13:34.190 --> 00:13:37.100
and threads uploading the
data at the same time, kind

00:13:37.100 --> 00:13:40.940
of saturating your network
as much as possible.

00:13:40.940 --> 00:13:42.860
We don't have any
network throttling,

00:13:42.860 --> 00:13:45.620
so you have to kind of use
other tricks like, trickle,

00:13:45.620 --> 00:13:49.625
or enforce the bandwidth
utilization at the network

00:13:49.625 --> 00:13:51.660
layer.

00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:55.350
If you're concerned about
POSIX metadata at all,

00:13:55.350 --> 00:13:57.910
there's a -P option
that was recently

00:13:57.910 --> 00:14:00.750
introduced to capture the
POSIX attributes of your source

00:14:00.750 --> 00:14:03.810
files, and stored
it as a GCS metadata

00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:05.380
as part of the
object that's in GCS.

00:14:09.130 --> 00:14:12.550
So gsutil itself is a
relatively basic tool.

00:14:12.550 --> 00:14:16.840
We don't do kind of like
network optimization.

00:14:16.840 --> 00:14:20.430
So if you want even
better transfer speeds,

00:14:20.430 --> 00:14:25.460
we have partners to [INAUDIBLE],
Cloud Fastpath, Aspera,

00:14:25.460 --> 00:14:25.960
BitSpeed.

00:14:25.960 --> 00:14:30.970
So some of them they do
UDP or TCB optimization

00:14:30.970 --> 00:14:31.810
for better speeds.

00:14:34.820 --> 00:14:37.130
All right so what
if your data sources

00:14:37.130 --> 00:14:40.030
are at locations with a low
bandwidth or no bandwidth

00:14:40.030 --> 00:14:40.700
at all?

00:14:40.700 --> 00:14:44.820
So the solution is
offline data transfer,

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:50.070
or offline import-export,
well, import in this case.

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:53.150
So what this means is that
you have a physical storage

00:14:53.150 --> 00:14:55.640
media with the hard drives,
so storage appliance.

00:14:55.640 --> 00:14:59.810
You load the data and then ship
the hardware to an injection

00:14:59.810 --> 00:15:01.400
point with good network.

00:15:01.400 --> 00:15:07.040
And someone there will ingest
the data to GCS for you.

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:10.730
We have several third
party providers to do this.

00:15:10.730 --> 00:15:14.369
We launched this collaboration
about a year and a half ago.

00:15:14.369 --> 00:15:16.160
So in North America,
we have Iron Mountain.

00:15:16.160 --> 00:15:17.750
You can see the pricing here.

00:15:17.750 --> 00:15:22.010
In EMEA and APAC we're
Prime Focus Technologies.

00:15:22.010 --> 00:15:26.480
So we have a bunch of
customers using these services.

00:15:26.480 --> 00:15:29.420
Customers essentially sign up
with these third-party service

00:15:29.420 --> 00:15:32.060
providers, separately
from Google.

00:15:32.060 --> 00:15:35.570
Now a lot of use
cases where data

00:15:35.570 --> 00:15:40.520
is generated offline, for
example, at C, collected

00:15:40.520 --> 00:15:43.442
on hard drives, so when the
ship reaches port, what they do

00:15:43.442 --> 00:15:45.650
is that they ship the hard
drive so Iron Mountain get

00:15:45.650 --> 00:15:47.180
the data ingested.

00:15:47.180 --> 00:15:48.854
It's not necessarily
a lot of data,

00:15:48.854 --> 00:15:51.020
sometimes we are talking
about gigabytes or hundreds

00:15:51.020 --> 00:15:53.260
of gigabytes of data.

00:15:53.260 --> 00:15:56.570
But you know, when
you're out at sea,

00:15:56.570 --> 00:15:58.240
generally you can't
even send one byte

00:15:58.240 --> 00:15:59.820
if you don't have network.

00:15:59.820 --> 00:16:03.030
So they kind of use
this offline method.

00:16:03.030 --> 00:16:06.410
Now what's new here
is that we just

00:16:06.410 --> 00:16:11.080
started working
with Zadara to offer

00:16:11.080 --> 00:16:13.870
offline migration of live data.

00:16:13.870 --> 00:16:15.860
They have a booth
downstairs if you guys want

00:16:15.860 --> 00:16:17.650
to talk to them about this.

00:16:17.650 --> 00:16:20.290
So how this works is
that they will send you

00:16:20.290 --> 00:16:22.180
like two appliances.

00:16:22.180 --> 00:16:24.710
You capture the data
onto the appliances.

00:16:24.710 --> 00:16:27.620
You ship one to the Zadara.

00:16:27.620 --> 00:16:29.780
They will upload to GCS.

00:16:29.780 --> 00:16:31.910
And then they will use
the second appliance

00:16:31.910 --> 00:16:35.720
to do synchronization of
data that has changed.

00:16:35.720 --> 00:16:39.680
So if you're thinking about
the hard drive approach,

00:16:39.680 --> 00:16:42.260
basically you're taking a
snapshot loading of their data,

00:16:42.260 --> 00:16:44.300
loading into the hard
drive, shipping it out.

00:16:44.300 --> 00:16:46.060
But what if your data evolves?

00:16:46.060 --> 00:16:48.444
So Zadara kind of
solves that problem.

00:16:48.444 --> 00:16:50.360
And because they are
using storage appliances,

00:16:50.360 --> 00:16:52.360
you could capture
a lot more data

00:16:52.360 --> 00:16:56.720
than on an individual
10 terabyte hard drive.

00:16:56.720 --> 00:16:59.940
So as a summary, so we
have Transfer Service

00:16:59.940 --> 00:17:03.390
for Cloud Data, gsutil
and partner products

00:17:03.390 --> 00:17:09.520
for data in locations
with good network.

00:17:09.520 --> 00:17:15.730
If you don't have a lot of data,
say under 100 terabytes or so,

00:17:15.730 --> 00:17:18.040
you can use like
hard drive imports,

00:17:18.040 --> 00:17:21.220
because if you have more data,
then the number of hard drives

00:17:21.220 --> 00:17:23.962
become kind of unmanageable.

00:17:23.962 --> 00:17:25.420
And we have more
data or live data,

00:17:25.420 --> 00:17:27.490
then there's Zadara for you.

00:17:27.490 --> 00:17:31.860
All right, so let's
cover structured data.

00:17:31.860 --> 00:17:34.200
Now structured data is
more a partner play.

00:17:34.200 --> 00:17:37.050
So we have a number of partners
who can help you migrate data

00:17:37.050 --> 00:17:40.620
from on premise
databases, or even

00:17:40.620 --> 00:17:45.000
other Cloud Databases to
Cloud SQL and Spanner.

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:48.690
So we have Informatica, Xplenty,
support both flavors of Cloud

00:17:48.690 --> 00:17:51.690
SQL, MySQL and Postgres.

00:17:51.690 --> 00:17:55.770
Segment, Stitch, Talend, and
Alooma support Postgres, SQL,

00:17:55.770 --> 00:17:58.220
and Dbvisit, Attunity
supports MySQL only.

00:18:01.770 --> 00:18:04.290
Similarly, if you are
interested in importing data

00:18:04.290 --> 00:18:08.910
into Dataproc, we have a whole
bunch of partners for that.

00:18:08.910 --> 00:18:13.320
And depending on where
you want to land data,

00:18:13.320 --> 00:18:14.904
whether on GCS or
persistent disk,

00:18:14.904 --> 00:18:16.445
we have different
partners supporting

00:18:16.445 --> 00:18:17.520
the different targets.

00:18:21.840 --> 00:18:24.030
So finally BigQuery data
integration partner,

00:18:24.030 --> 00:18:27.220
and so again, partner
play, right generally,

00:18:27.220 --> 00:18:30.550
everything from where
[INAUDIBLE] data sources.

00:18:30.550 --> 00:18:34.780
So those marketing CRM
data sources, databases

00:18:34.780 --> 00:18:38.614
like MySQL, SQL server,
Postgres, Oracle,

00:18:38.614 --> 00:18:42.110
or data warehouses
like Teradata,

00:18:42.110 --> 00:18:43.895
particularly [INAUDIBLE].

00:18:47.460 --> 00:18:50.340
Now BigQuery is becoming
very popular with marketeers,

00:18:50.340 --> 00:18:54.924
so you can use BigQuery to
analyze the click rates,

00:18:54.924 --> 00:18:56.340
and all that to
kind of figure out

00:18:56.340 --> 00:19:01.830
like whether you're advertising
campaign is effective.

00:19:01.830 --> 00:19:06.510
So we just announced BigQuery
data Transfer Service.

00:19:06.510 --> 00:19:09.240
And that basically
allows you to import data

00:19:09.240 --> 00:19:13.890
from Google Ad Platforms
into BigQuery for analysis.

00:19:13.890 --> 00:19:17.070
Now we also support other
sources of marketing data,

00:19:17.070 --> 00:19:20.580
so whether Adobe Analytics,
Facebook, Twitter and so on,

00:19:20.580 --> 00:19:22.810
through our panel ecosystem.

00:19:22.810 --> 00:19:25.292
So you can check
out the link here.

00:19:25.292 --> 00:19:28.140
This basically is pretty
update it to kind of like

00:19:28.140 --> 00:19:31.890
give you an idea of which
partners are available to do

00:19:31.890 --> 00:19:33.960
this data import.

00:19:33.960 --> 00:19:37.380
And then reach out
to your rep as well

00:19:37.380 --> 00:19:40.870
if you have additional needs.

00:19:40.870 --> 00:19:45.450
So I've covered unstructured
data and structured data.

00:19:45.450 --> 00:19:47.540
Let's talk about IT
workflow perspective.

00:19:47.540 --> 00:19:50.365
This is actually a summary
of the talk I had yesterday,

00:19:50.365 --> 00:19:53.820
just about 3, 4 slides.

00:19:53.820 --> 00:19:55.710
So IT workflows are
generally things

00:19:55.710 --> 00:20:00.710
like bursting, caching,
backup disaster recovery,

00:20:00.710 --> 00:20:02.440
curing and archiving.

00:20:02.440 --> 00:20:08.250
How can IT start running
these standard workflows?

00:20:08.250 --> 00:20:12.050
How can they get
data onto the Cloud?

00:20:12.050 --> 00:20:14.160
So Cloud Bursting, what's that?

00:20:14.160 --> 00:20:16.220
So Cloud Bursting
is essentially you

00:20:16.220 --> 00:20:19.220
are running
workloads on premise,

00:20:19.220 --> 00:20:21.360
in your own data centers.

00:20:21.360 --> 00:20:23.850
And you want to take
advantage of what

00:20:23.850 --> 00:20:26.510
we can offer in the Cloud
for compute, for example

00:20:26.510 --> 00:20:31.590
like low cost,
scale, even failover.

00:20:31.590 --> 00:20:33.360
And then you care
about making sure

00:20:33.360 --> 00:20:35.040
that the data that
is on premise is

00:20:35.040 --> 00:20:37.410
available to the compute
workloads in the Cloud.

00:20:37.410 --> 00:20:40.680
And then you don't have the
big hassle of explicitly

00:20:40.680 --> 00:20:42.990
moving data around.

00:20:42.990 --> 00:20:45.620
We partner with
Avere, for example.

00:20:45.620 --> 00:20:47.640
They are really
great for compute,

00:20:47.640 --> 00:20:52.580
high performance compute because
they allow you to store data

00:20:52.580 --> 00:20:56.100
on premise, make the data
available for computing

00:20:56.100 --> 00:20:58.650
instances in the Cloud
or vice versa, which

00:20:58.650 --> 00:21:01.950
is you have data in the Cloud
taking advantage of low cost

00:21:01.950 --> 00:21:04.770
GCS, and then
allowing on premise

00:21:04.770 --> 00:21:07.510
compute to access the
data that is in the Cloud.

00:21:10.270 --> 00:21:12.810
So to talk about backup, so
if you have an existing backup

00:21:12.810 --> 00:21:16.690
solutions, you can
very, very easily

00:21:16.690 --> 00:21:21.010
retarget your backup
destination to the Cloud.

00:21:21.010 --> 00:21:23.190
So we work with
partners like Veritas

00:21:23.190 --> 00:21:25.980
and so on, and its
just a UI change.

00:21:25.980 --> 00:21:30.780
You can see the example
here on the right.

00:21:30.780 --> 00:21:33.720
And then if you don't have
existing backup solutions,

00:21:33.720 --> 00:21:36.060
we again, we are working
with a lot of our partners

00:21:36.060 --> 00:21:38.730
that will support us today.

00:21:38.730 --> 00:21:41.760
So you can talk to them.

00:21:41.760 --> 00:21:43.590
If you don't have
existing backup solutions

00:21:43.590 --> 00:21:46.480
but then your workflow or
data set is very simple,

00:21:46.480 --> 00:21:48.870
then you can use like
Cloud Gateway Solutions.

00:21:48.870 --> 00:21:52.700
Again, we have a lot of partners
that will support you on that.

00:21:52.700 --> 00:21:54.020
Cloud-tiering is interesting.

00:21:54.020 --> 00:21:56.390
So there are two ways of
looking at Cloud-tiering.

00:21:56.390 --> 00:21:58.765
Well generally one way of
looking at Cloud-tiering, which

00:21:58.765 --> 00:22:03.030
is you have data on
premise, storage where

00:22:03.030 --> 00:22:04.596
you hold all of your data.

00:22:04.596 --> 00:22:06.220
Now the problem with
on-premise storage

00:22:06.220 --> 00:22:07.620
is that it is very expensive.

00:22:07.620 --> 00:22:09.690
So why not move
some of that data

00:22:09.690 --> 00:22:13.050
to Cloud, to take
advantage of our scale

00:22:13.050 --> 00:22:16.600
and low cost, especially
like Coldline for example?

00:22:16.600 --> 00:22:22.200
And so you can make space
for an additional live data

00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:23.370
on premise.

00:22:23.370 --> 00:22:25.450
So we have a partner
called Comprise

00:22:25.450 --> 00:22:30.880
that can do an analysis of
your data usage on premise.

00:22:30.880 --> 00:22:34.390
And they can identify which
data is kind of like cold data.

00:22:34.390 --> 00:22:38.100
And it can then help you move
that data across to the Cloud,

00:22:38.100 --> 00:22:40.700
based on policy and so on.

00:22:40.700 --> 00:22:44.970
Another way of doing it is if
you are onboarding new storage

00:22:44.970 --> 00:22:46.730
devices or storage
technologies, you

00:22:46.730 --> 00:22:51.660
can go with the on premise
object storage vendors,

00:22:51.660 --> 00:22:53.820
like Cloud [INAUDIBLE].

00:22:53.820 --> 00:22:56.540
And they do automatic
tiering, and so

00:22:56.540 --> 00:22:59.280
making sure that the
colder data automatically

00:22:59.280 --> 00:23:02.750
gets tiered to GCS.

00:23:02.750 --> 00:23:05.190
So a summary here is
that for whatever kind of

00:23:05.190 --> 00:23:10.310
IT workflows that you have, and
if you want to leverage a Cloud

00:23:10.310 --> 00:23:12.650
or even explore moving some
of the data to the Cloud,

00:23:12.650 --> 00:23:17.070
we have the whole ecosystem
of partners for that.

